<section class="page-head">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
    <h1>A nurse call is used for everything and says nothing.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The bell was designed for one thing: getting help. It has since become the only channel a
      patient has, so every kind of request now travels down it — medical, practical, social — and
      arrives stripped of everything except a room number.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="shell split split--sticky">
    <div>
      <h2>Four consequences</h2>
      <p>
        These are not edge cases. They are what an ordinary shift looks like, and they are the
        reason wards lose time in small pieces that never show up in any report.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <div class="rows">
        <div class="rows__item">
          <div>
            <h3>Request and qualification do not match</h3>
            <span class="rows__role">Consequence 01</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Every type of request — medical, support, non-medical — arrives as the same alert, so
            there is no way to anticipate who should go. Either an overqualified colleague handles
            something anyone could have done, or an underqualified one spends valuable minutes
            finding the person who can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rows__item">
          <div>
            <h3>The room has to be left again</h3>
            <span class="rows__role">Consequence 02</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Nobody can bring equipment or medication for a request they have not heard yet. So the
            visit becomes two visits, and the corridor between the room and the station gets walked
            twice for a single need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rows__item">
          <div>
            <h3>Calls that were never meant to happen</h3>
            <span class="rows__role">Consequence 03</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Buttons get pressed by accident — leaning on them, catching them in bedding. Because an
            accidental call looks exactly like an urgent one, somebody still has to walk over and
            confirm that nothing is wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rows__item">
          <div>
            <h3>Interruptions land where they are dangerous</h3>
            <span class="rows__role">Consequence 04</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Preparing medication or working on a line demands uninterrupted attention, and
            interruption during those tasks is associated with higher error rates. On top of that,
            the constant monotone of the bell itself is a measurable source of noise and stress on a
            busy shift.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--soft">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it adds up</p>
      <h2>Nobody loses an hour. Everybody loses two minutes, all day.</h2>
      <p>
        Each of these costs a couple of minutes. Multiplied by roughly eight calls per patient per
        day across a whole ward, across a whole year, the arithmetic stops being small — and the
        time comes out of the same team that is already short.
      </p>
    </div>
